WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
The objective, precisely.

2
00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:08.365
Take one training example and render it with the model’s chat template into a token sequence x of length T.

3
00:00:08.645 --> 00:00:19.184
A causal language model with parameters theta gives, at each position t, a distribution over the vocabulary for the next token, conditioned on everything before it.

4
00:00:19.464 --> 00:00:27.144
The per-token loss at position t is the negative log-probability the model assigned to the token that actually comes next.

5
00:00:27.424 --> 00:00:32.587
Now let M be the set of positions whose next token you want to learn (the “trained” positions).

6
00:00:32.867 --> 00:00:37.816
The SFT loss for the example is the average of those per-token losses over M.

7
00:00:38.096 --> 00:00:44.368
For a batch, trainers average over all trained positions in the batch, so every scored token counts equally.

8
00:00:44.648 --> 00:00:55.549
TRL’s documentation describes the loss as token-level cross-entropy and notes the one-position shift: the model at position t is scored on token t + 1.

9
00:00:55.829 --> 00:00:57.877
Two equivalent views are worth having.

10
00:00:58.157 --> 00:01:04.024
Statistically, minimizing this loss is maximum likelihood estimation of theta on your data.

11
00:01:04.304 --> 00:01:17.552
Information-theoretically, the expected per-token loss is the cross-entropy between the data’s next-token distribution and the model’s, which equals the data’s own entropy plus the KL divergence from data to model.

12
00:01:17.832 --> 00:01:25.469
The entropy term is fixed by your data, so training reduces the KL term, and the loss can’t go below the entropy of the data.

13
00:01:25.749 --> 00:01:37.611
That floor is why a loss of exactly zero on real text is a warning sign, not an achievement (for templated, deterministic targets like JSON labels, the floor really is near zero).

14
00:01:37.891 --> 00:01:41.325
The loss is in nats per token when natural logs are used.

15
00:01:41.605 --> 00:01:47.131
Its exponential is perplexity, the effective number of equally likely choices per token.

16
00:01:47.411 --> 00:01:48.584
A loss of 0.

17
00:01:48.864 --> 00:01:51.125
7 means a perplexity of about 2.
